      text: "Ask most adults how many close friends they have, and the answer is likely to be smaller than they would like. It is not that we fall out with people; dramatic betrayals and bitter rifts are surprisingly rare. More often, friends simply drift apart. A job in another city, a new baby or a demanding career gradually reduces contact until one day we realise that we have not spoken for years to someone who once knew us better than anyone. Researchers have identified three conditions that make close friendships form most easily: proximity, repeated unplanned interaction, and settings in which people feel free to let their guard down. University and early adulthood provide all three in abundance, which is why so many lifelong friendships begin there. In middle age, by contrast, our time is heavily scheduled, and spontaneous encounters become rare. If we had met our closest friends at thirty-five rather than at nineteen, many of us would probably not be friends with them at all. Time is another factor. One widely cited study estimated that it takes around fifty hours together to move from acquaintance to casual friend, and more than two hundred to become close friends. That is an enormous investment for someone juggling work and family. It is hardly surprising that friendships are often the first thing to be sacrificed when life gets busy, or that we tend to take them for granted until they are gone. Yet the costs of neglecting friendship are considerable. Loneliness has been linked to higher rates of heart disease, depression and even early death, with some researchers comparing its impact to that of smoking. Strong social bonds, on the other hand, appear to protect us against stress and help us recover more quickly from illness. So how can busy adults keep their friendships alive? Psychologists offer some practical suggestions. First, build rituals: a monthly dinner, a weekly phone call, a shared hobby. Regularity removes the need to organise each meeting from scratch. Second, don't wait for the perfect moment. A short message saying you thought of someone is far better than silence. Third, be willing to show vulnerability. Friendships deepen not when we share our successes, but when we confide in each other about our worries and failures. Finally, if a friendship has gone through a rough patch, it may be worth making the first move. Many of us hold a grudge long after we have forgotten the original reason for it. A single honest conversation can patch things up more effectively than years of polite avoidance. After all, the friend you reconnect with today might be the one who stands by you in ten years' time.",
